  overview:
    "The first two weeks of this module established the inner terrain of self-leadership: you mapped your emotional intelligence baseline and began developing the self-regulation practices that allow you to choose your responses rather than enact your impulses. This week moves deeper into that same inner territory — to the values that underlie your decisions, and to the frameworks that help you navigate situations where those values are tested or in conflict. Bill George, former CEO of Medtronic and the architect of authentic leadership theory, argues that a leader's values are not their aspirations — they are their behavior under pressure. It is easy to commit to integrity, transparency, and fairness in stable conditions. The real test arrives when honesty will cost you a relationship, when fairness requires a decision that is unpopular, when staying clean means watching a competitor who is willing to cut corners win a contract you needed. Ethical decision-making is not a separate skill from leadership — it is the application of your values to the moments that reveal what those values actually are. This week you will learn three major ethical frameworks used in philosophy and applied business ethics, develop a method for identifying and articulating your own core values with the specificity that makes them operational, and work through a structured decision process that applies when your values conflict with each other or with external pressure. The Asanov story this week brings Aibek to the most consequential decision Taza Foods has faced — one that will determine not just a transaction but the kind of company they intend to be.",
