        "Research by James Rest and Muriel Bebeau on moral development in professional contexts, and by Linda Klebe Treviño on ethical organizational culture, converges on a finding that has significant implications for leaders of growing businesses: individual ethical behavior is profoundly shaped by the ethical environment of the organization. Treviño's research identifies two primary mechanisms through which leaders shape ethical culture. The first is the ethical role modeling of the leader themselves — what they do, how they make decisions, what they reward and punish, and crucially, how they behave when no one is watching. The second is the formal and informal systems the leader creates or tolerates: whether the compensation structure incentivizes corner-cutting, whether concerns can be raised without retaliation, whether stated values are actually referenced in real decisions or function only as wall art. Treviño's concept of the 'ethical climate' — the shared perception of organizational practices and procedures that have ethical content — predicts ethical behavior more reliably than individual moral development does. The leader who builds ethical systems, not just ethical intentions, is the one whose organization behaves ethically at scale. For Aibek and Aigul, at the stage of growth where Taza Foods is now hiring people beyond their immediate circle of trust, this transition from personal ethics to systemic ethics is the most urgent organizational development challenge they face.",
